{
  "title": "Sliding Window Maximum",
  "description": "Largest value inside every window of fixed size k.",
  "statement": "A metrics collector samples a stream and a dashboard must show the peak value of every window of `k` consecutive samples.\n\nYou are given an array and a window size `k`. Report the maximum of each window, in order, so the output contains exactly `n - k + 1` values.\n\nRescanning each window is quadratic. A monotonic deque holds the indexes of values that could still become a window maximum: when a new value arrives, every smaller value at the back can be discarded forever, because the new value is at least as large and leaves the window no earlier. The front of the deque is then always the current maximum, and each index is added and removed at most once.",
  "inputFormat": "The input is read from standard input as exactly three lines:\n\n1. Line 1: integer `n` - the number of values (`1 <= n <= 100000`).\n2. Line 2: `n` space-separated integers `nums[0] ... nums[n-1]`.\n3. Line 3: integer `k` - the window size (`1 <= k <= n`).",
  "outputFormat": "Print a single line containing exactly `n - k + 1` space-separated integers: the maximum of each window, left to right.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= n <= 10^5",
    "1 <= k <= n",
    "-10^9 <= nums[i] <= 10^9",
    "The output always contains exactly n - k + 1 numbers"
  ],
  "notes": "Equal values do not improve on each other, so the back of the deque can drop values that are smaller **or equal** to the incoming value - that is what stops duplicate runs from bloating the structure. Stale indexes must leave the front before it is used as the answer, otherwise a window can report a value that has already slid out. `k = 1` returns the array itself and `k = n` returns a single value; both are in the test data.",
  "difficulty": "HARD",
  "tags": [
    "array",
    "sliding-window",
    "monotonic-stack",
    "queue"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 1000,
  "memoryLimitKb": 256000,
  "starterCode": {
    "python": "import sys\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    n = int(data[0])\n    nums = [int(x) for x in data[1:1 + n]]\n    k = int(data[1 + n])\n    # TODO: print the result\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n",
    "javascript": "const fs = require('fs');\nconst data = fs.readFileSync(0, 'utf8').trim().split(/\\s+/).filter((t) => t.length > 0);\nconst n = parseInt(data[0], 10);\nconst nums = data.slice(1, 1 + n).map(Number);\nconst k = parseInt(data[1 + n], 10);\n// TODO: print the result\n",
    "java": "import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = sc.nextInt();\n        int[] nums = new int[n];\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            nums[i] = sc.nextInt();\n        }\n        int k = sc.nextInt();\n        // TODO: print the result\n        sc.close();\n    }\n}\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);\n    cin.tie(nullptr);\n\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    vector<int> nums(n);\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        cin >> nums[i];\n    }\n    int k;\n    cin >> k;\n    // TODO: print the result\n    return 0;\n}\n"
  },
